+       </para>
+
+       <para>
+        A chain of explicit joins passes the limit once per
+        <literal>JOIN</literal>, but the chain produces a single report: the
+        one for its outermost construct, whose <literal>FROM</literal> items
+        include those of all the others.  Constructs that do not enclose one
+        another are reported separately, so a statement whose join order is
+        constrained in several independent places produces one report for
+        each of them.
+       </para>
+
+       <para>
+        This is useful for detecting queries whose join order the planner is
+        no longer free to optimize.  Note that, because the check occurs
+        during planning, it applies to any query that is planned, including
+        those run through <application>PL/pgSQL</application> and other
+        <acronym>SPI</acronym> callers, but not to reuse of an already-cached
